namespace Multiplayer.Network.Sync
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Stable wire ids for every synced SURFACE on the unified <see cref="SurfaceRouter"/> — the
    /// surfaceId byte of the 0x67 envelope. Geoscape partition = 0xA0-0xBF (tactical rides 0x80-0x9F).
    /// Never reuse a retired id: a new sender on one would silently mis-route on an old peer.
    /// The DIAGNOSTIC band 0xC0-0xCF sits outside both partitions on purpose: nothing on it is domain
    /// state, nothing on it is applied, and nothing on it is sent at all unless <c>MpDiag.On</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ─── RETIRED / RESERVED ids — permanent tombstones, do NOT reuse ─────────────────
    ///   • ids 1-30 / 60-79 (action surfaces) + 1-10 (state channels) — the old repo's per-kind
    ///     migration catalog (mirrored its SyncedActionIds/ChannelIds). Never emitted by this repo;
    ///     constants deleted 2026-07-22. The live partition starts at 0xA0.
    ///   • 0xA0 GeoWallet / 0xA1 GeoState — retired: wallet + per-channel state ride the generic
    ///     value rail 0xAC (GeoRail); no sender remains.
    ///   • 0xAA GeoResearch — retired 2026-07-26 (constant deleted same day): the bespoke research
    ///     side channel; research rides the 0xAC value rail + the 0xAB intent surface only.
    ///   • 0xB2 GeoScrapCart — retired 2026-07-26 (constant deleted same day): the shared scrap
    ///     cart's bespoke echo; the cart rides the generic value rail as mod root "M#cart".
    ///   • 0x85 TacAim / 0x86 TacAimIntent — retired 2026-08-04 (constants deleted same day, arc A8
    ///     REMOVED by developer decision): the committed manual-aim stance was mirrored as SHARED
    ///     last-writer-wins state per soldier. Reverted to law 5's default — manual aim is a
    ///     per-window LOCAL mode again, like hover/preview aiming and every other pose. Its
    ///     `TacticalAimSync` family, its per-frame sampler and RailCheck L82 went with it. The
    ///     desync it was built for (a peer already holding `TravelType.Aim` skips
    ///     `FireWeaponAtTargetCrt`:1649-1683's aim-start block while mirrors play the entry-into-aim
    ///     animation first) is ACCEPTED as cosmetic. Same tombstone rule as the two 0x82/0x83 ops
    ///     A8's first attempt (3071859) burned: never re-mint these ids.
    /// </remarks>
    public static class SurfaceIds
    {
        // ─── Tactical band 0x80-0x9F (arc A2 opens it; A1 needed none — entry rides the save transfer) ───
        public const byte TacTurn = 0x80;  // TURN CURSOR + MISSION END host→all: [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = turn(1, [factionDefGuid:string][turnNumber:i32] — the PRE-increment number both peers read at TacMission.OnNewTurn, since PlayTurnCrt is what does the +1) / end(2, [playerFactionState:u8] = the host's TacFactionState for the Player participant, the ONE authoritative outcome bit the native battle-summary branches on). ONE surface for both ops on purpose: a single seq stream is what makes it impossible for a stale turn message to overtake the game-over (they are strictly ordered against each other, which two surfaces could not guarantee). Host→all only — the client's answer to it is the 0x81 end-turn intent. op = readyTally(4, [readyCount:i32][totalCount:i32]) — the ADVISORY "how many players say they have finished moving" aggregate, added 2026-08-05. It is a LABEL and nothing else: no code path reads either number to gate, delay or trigger anything (RailCheck L119 asserts that as a set equality over the readers plus an EXECUTED probe of both tactical arbiters), because a count that decides anything is a quorum and laws L84/L91 forbid one. It rides THIS surface rather than a new one for the same reason turn and end share it: a stale tally must never overtake the turn edge that resets it, and only one seq stream can guarantee that. Per-peer flags are NOT on the wire and NOT on the rail — they live on SessionManager.ClientInfo.TacReady (peer bookkeeping is the lobby's job; L91 arm (c) forbids a rail type from holding a peer-id collection at all), and a PAUSED peer keeps its seat in totalCount while counting as not-ready.
        public const byte TacTurnIntent = 0x81;  // END-TURN client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][factionDefGuid:string], op = endTurn(1)); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup; host validates off its OWN turn cursor (TacticalTurnSync.Validate — the guid must BE the host's CurrentFaction, that faction must be player-controlled and actually playing) + executes NATIVELY (TacticalFaction.RequestEndTurn, the same call the host's own button makes), outcome returns to every peer as the 0x80 turn op. op = setReady(3, [ready:u8]) — the ADVISORY "I have finished moving this round" flag, added 2026-08-05; NO validator and no reject, because the intent spends nothing, orders nothing and names no entity, so the only possible answer is "recorded" (the same posture as the 0x88 aim-pose intent). The host records it on that peer's own SessionManager row and answers with the 0x80 op-4 tally. It rides this family because it is the same question one step earlier than the end-turn it sits above. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte TacCommand = 0x82;  // PER-SOLDIER COMMAND host→all: [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = activate(1, [actorKey:i32][abilityDefGuid:string][hasTarget:u8]([TacticalAbilityTarget fieldmask+fields, TacAbilityTargetCodec] only when set)[fumbled:u8]) — the ORDER, mirrored so every peer PLAYS it natively for presentation (Arc-4's "ship the ORDER, never the pose"; the path is re-derived by each peer's own deterministic Pathfinder) / settle(2, [actorKey:i32][posX:f32][posY:f32][posZ:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32][forced:u8][statusCount:i32]([statusDefName:string][refActorKey:i32])* — the STATUS SET, added 2026-08-05, keyed by def NAME because TFTV mints status defs with Guid.NewGuid() (the 0x8A argument verbatim) and carrying the one actor a status may name, read structurally off the status type's first TacticalActorBase field, which is where MountedStatus.VehicleActorBase lives. Statuses were the last part of an actor that rode nothing at all: they existed on each peer only as a side effect of replaying the ability that applied them, so a dropped, queued, refused or torn order left a status the host had and a peer did not, permanently — and the vehicle passenger roster IS that hole, since VehicleComponent.Passengers is written only from MountedStatus.OnApply/OnUnapply:41/:95. It rides the settle, not a surface of its own, because the settle is already one actor's authoritative closer AND already sweeps every keyed live actor at the turn edge, so the set is re-asserted routinely rather than only when a peer notices a hole in the 0x84 stream) — the host-authoritative CLOSER after the action really ended on the host, because the one thing a peer CANNOT reproduce is the cost: Move_AbilityDef.ActionPointCost is 0 so TacticalAbility.ApplyCosts:931-944 charges nothing, and the real charge happens once at end of traversal (TacticalNavigationComponent.cs:800 ActionPoints.Subtract(distTravelled / DistanceToAPFactor)) against an interrupt-dependent distance (overwatch / StopReason.EnemySeen, snapped via MoveAbility.SnapPointAndDistance:48-69). Position rides with it because actors are dynamic nav obstacles (the mover disables its own at MoveAbility.cs:108) so the obstacle set — and hence the path — can differ per peer under simultaneous commands; the closer makes any such divergence cosmetic and self-correcting on arrival. ONE surface for both ops on purpose, exactly as 0x80 carries turn+end: a single seq stream is what makes it impossible for a settle to overtake its own activate. Host→all only; the client's answer to it is the 0x83 command intent. A5 CARRIES THE ENEMY ON THIS SAME OP AND TOOK NO NEW SURFACE: an enemy action is the same TacticalAbility.Activate an order is (every one of the twelve PhoenixPoint.Tactical.AI.Actions classes reaches TacticalAbility.ExecuteAndWait:1168, which is three lines over Activate:1078, and AI movement is the same MoveAbility a player click uses), so the host simply mirrors EVERY faction instead of only the player's. Two consequences ride here: the rider WHITELIST became a declared DROP list (the AI executes data-configured ability defs, which no whitelist can enumerate — TacticalCommandSync.LocalAbilities names the seven the game itself treats as ambient), and an AUTONOMOUS activation — one the engine raised rather than a peer ordering it, recognised by TacticalAbilityTarget.AttackType != Regular, i.e. exactly Overwatch/ReturnFire/ZoneControl/Synced — NEVER crosses in either direction, because every peer raises its own from the same replicated board and a mirrored copy would be a SECOND overwatch shot. A7 ADDS OP 3, select(3, [actorKey:i32][equipmentDefGuid:string — "" is "select nothing", which RagdollDieAbility:162 really does]) — SWITCHING A WEAPON IS NOT AN ABILITY, so A3a's prefix on TacticalAbility.Activate could never see it: the model funnel is EquipmentComponent.SetSelectedEquipment:242 (it raises the game's own EquipmentChangedEvent:266) and the tactical UI clicks it straight from UIStateCharacterSelected:748/751, UIStateShoot:854/862 and UIStateAbilitySelected:725/736. Un-relayed it was not merely cosmetic: the HOST validates every order with the game's GetDisabledState(), whose EquipmentNotSelected arm (TacticalAbility.GetDisabledStateInternal:435 -> isEquipmentOfSelectedGroup:481-499) tests the HOST's selection, so a client that switched locally had its next grenade or reload REJECTED. The seam is a non-blocking PREFIX (the same method is the game's own repair path — EquipmentComponent:56/102/118/272, RagdollDieAbility:162 — so blocking a client would leave it holding nothing the first time a weapon broke). The settle op also gained a trailing [forced:u8]: a settle the host sent BECAUSE it refused an order is applied immediately instead of waiting for the refused peer's stuck speculative ability to end. A settle is deliberately NOT applied the instant it lands — the receiving peer may still be playing the mirrored move, whose own nav would overwrite the snap silently, so it is held until that actor stops executing (TacticalCommandSync.ClientTick).
        public const byte TacResult = 0x84;  // RESOLVED-ATTACK RESULTS host→all (arc A3b): [seq:u32][op:u8][body], seq rides SurfaceSeq. op = damage(1, [actorKey:i32][slotName:string — "" is the actor itself, anything else is a body part named by IDamageReceiver.GetSlotName(); ItemSlotDef.SlotName is a def field so it is identical on every peer and TacticalActor.ValidateActor:1178-1186 makes the game itself assert it is unique per actor][DamageResult fieldmask+fields, DamageResultCodec][recvHp:f32][recvArmor:f32][actorHp:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32][dead:u8]) / resnap(2, [count:i32] then per actor [key:i32][hp:f32][ap:f32][wp:f32][dead:u8](A5: [lootCount:i32]([itemDefGuid:string][destroy:u8])* only when dead — the host's ARCHIVED corpse manifest, so a resnapshot-forced death drops what the host's did instead of keeping everything)[slotCount:i32]([slotName:string][hp:f32][armor:f32])*[statusCount:i32]([statusDefName:string][refActorKey:i32])* — the same STATUS SET the 0x82 settle carries and for the same reason: hp/ap/wp/dead/slots were never the whole of an actor, so the recovery path could not repair the one class of divergence no re-emit heals) / A4's ACTOR LIFECYCLE pair, on THIS surface and not a new one: spawn(3, [key:i32][componentSetDefGuid:string][factionDefGuid:string][participant:u8][pos xyz:f32][rot xyzw:f32][sourceDeployZoneName:string]) and death(4, [key:i32][lootCount:i32]([itemDefGuid:string][destroy:u8])*). WHY THEY RIDE 0x84: a spawn and a death have exactly this surface's shape — discrete, unrepeatable, unhealable by re-emit — so they inherit its contiguity check and its resnapshot recovery for free; and ONE seq stream is the only thing that stops a damage record from overtaking the spawn of the actor it names (the same argument that puts turn+end on 0x80 and activate+settle on 0x82). Two surfaces could not order against each other. THE SPAWN KEY IS HOST-ASSIGNED (TacticalActorKey.AssignHostKey) and every other peer adopts it verbatim: a DERIVED key is a function of a board both peers snapshotted and a mid-battle spawn was in nobody's snapshot, so no derived scheme can name it. The client rebuilds through the game's OWN TacticalDeployZone.SpawnActor:257 with a null instance-data template (the def IS a just-spawned actor's state; anything later is the damage/resnap ops' business), and its own deploy roll is gated off entirely (TacParticipantSpawn.DeployForTurn:397 draws SharedData.Random against live enemy positions the peers legitimately disagree about — aliens only move on the host). The death op's loot list is the host's pre-rolled corpse manifest (DieAbility.ShouldDestroyItem:187-200, one draw per item); when a death DOES come through damage the manifest rides on op 1 instead, because the mirror's death starts inside that very ApplyDamage and no later message could reach DropItems in time. Death itself is applied through the game's own trigger (Health.Set(0) -> OnHealthChange:616-622 -> Die), never a hand-rolled corpse; an evacuated actor is HIDDEN by the native ExitMissionAbility path riding the 0x82 command seam and is NEVER destroyed. WHY RESULTS AND NOT ROLLS: there is no shared or seedable generator anywhere in a battle — scatter (Weapon.cs:481/487/490), damage roll + shred (DamageEffectDef.cs:52/57), fumble (TacticalAbility.cs:1128) and jetjump all draw from the global unseeded UnityEngine.Random, and loot/spawns from SharedData.Random = new System.Random() (SharedData.cs:69); Random.InitState is used ONLY for map generation. Worse, a shot has NO hit/miss die at all — the outcome is a scattered trajectory plus a physics raycast, so two peers rolling independently would hit DIFFERENT TARGETS, not merely different numbers. THE STREAM IS DISCRETE, which is the one place this surface differs from every geoscape one: last-writer-wins only DEDUPS a re-delivery, and a genuine GAP is unrecoverable because there is no periodic re-emit to heal it — so the client checks seq CONTIGUITY, says so loudly, and asks the host for a full resnap through the 0x83 intent op resnap(2). Host→all only.
        public const byte TacCommandIntent = 0x83;  // PER-SOLDIER COMMAND client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][abilityDefGuid:string][hasTarget:u8]([target fieldmask+fields] only when set), op = activate(1)) plus A7's selectEquipment(4, [actorKey:i32][equipmentDefGuid:string]) — the client half of the weapon-switch funnel above; plus A3b's resnap(2, no body — "I lost 0x84 records, resend every actor's state"); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup. The ONE generic command op for the shared battle — no ownership model: any peer commands any soldier and the host arbitrates by ARRIVAL ORDER against a PURE validator (TacticalCommandSync.Validate — actor exists/alive, its faction is player-controlled and playing its turn, the ability exists and is a declared rider, the actor is not already executing an ability, the native disabled-state gate, and the AP/WP cost check). That AP/busy pair IS the arbiter for two peers commanding one soldier: the first spends, the second re-validates against post-first HOST state and is refused with IntentRail.Reject + nudge. No ownership table and no in-memory claim ledger (v1's died on every reload). The acting peer meanwhile plays its own click locally for latency-free presentation; the 0x82 settle is what makes that speculation safe. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte TacAimPose = 0x87;  // AIM POSE host→all (arc A8b): [seq:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][targetKey:i32], op = aim(1); targetKey 0 = not aiming. THE ANIMATOR STANCE ONLY — no camera, no UI, no view-state push (0x85/0x86 stay retired tombstones; that half was removed by developer decision and RailCheck L97 keeps it out). Shared LAST-WRITER-WINS state per soldier, not an order: `TacticalLevelController.FireWeaponAtTargetCrt`:1645 skips the whole aim-start block (:1647-1678, a checkpoint wait with a 5 s ceiling) when `TacticalActor.CurrentlyAiming` is true, and that property reads ONLY the animator integers `TravelType`/`ShootSegmentType` (TacticalActor.cs:228-238) — so a soldier already aiming on one peer fires instantly there while every mirror first plays the entry-into-aim, a built-in desync on EVERY shot. The mirror writes exactly those integers through the game's own nav-free entry `PathProcessorUtils.SetAimParams(AimLoop)`:81-84 / `SetNullNavParams`:91-94 → `SetParams`:67-74 (whose entire body is a loop of `animator.SetInteger`), after the clip bind `TacActorAnimActions.ActivateShootingClips` that FireWeaponAtTargetCrt:1566 does one line earlier; facing rides along as a LERP at the game's own 6f rate, never its endpoint. `IdleAbility` and every nav-reaching path stay FORBIDDEN to this arc (3071859's `IdleAbility.ForceRefresh` moved mirrored soldiers instead of posing them). Dedup is by SAMPLING the live top view state once per frame against the shared table, never by capturing edges. Host→all only; the client's answer is the 0x88 intent.
        public const byte TacAimPoseIntent = 0x88;  // AIM POSE client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][actorKey:i32][targetKey:i32], op = setAim(1)); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup. No validator: an aim stance spends nothing and orders nothing — arbitration IS arrival order at the host (law 7), which is what "last writer wins" means here. The host echoes to ALL peers on 0x87. Intent-only surface.

        public const byte TacEntryParams = 0x89;  // NATIVE TACTICAL ENTRY, EXPERIMENT (law L103), OFF by default (NativeTacticalEntry.Enabled=false — the save transfer remains the shipping entry). Bidirectional, one message per direction per entry, no seq: it is a single discrete hand-off, and a re-delivery is answered by the same build. op = entry(1, host→all, [siteId:i32][spawnSeed:i32][isInMist:u8][plotDefGuid:string][len:i32][TacticalGameParams bytes, the game's OWN configured Serializer via SerializerRoundtrip]) — the GENERATION RESULT, not a seed: GeoMission.PrepareTacticalGame:305 collapses the whole geoscape into one [SerializeType] TacticalGameParams (TacMissionData with every ActorDeployData, crates, reinforcement counts, and MissionId.CreateNew()'s guid which no seed could reach), so a client rebuilds plotDef.LevelSceneDef.Binding.CloneAndAddLevelParams(params) and re-enters the game's OWN GeoLevelController.LaunchTacticalGame:1387 instead of loading a megabyte-scale save. isInMist and the plot guid ride BESIDE the graph because the type marks them SerializeMode.DoNotSerialize and MapPlotInstanceData [NonSerialized] respectively (verified on TacticalGameParams.cs:41/48-49); the parcel layout is taken from the client's own replicated GeoSite.MapPlotInstanceData and only re-rolled from GeoMission:762's own recipe when the site carries none. op = fallback(2, client→host, [reason:string]) — "I cannot build this locally", which arms the UNTOUCHED save transfer at TacDeployReadyCapture. The spawn-window RNG bracket that goes with it (TacMission.RunMissionActivators + TacMission.OnLevelStart) is sound in vanilla and DEAD under TFTV, which re-seeds UnityEngine.Random from Stopwatch.GetTimestamp() in ~15 places including enemy selection for base defense — see NativeTacticalEntry's class doc.

        public const byte TacHint = 0x8A;  // CONTEXT-HELP HINT any-peer→all: [hintDefName:string]. THE NAME AND NOTHING ELSE. A hint fires from each peer's OWN vision — TacContextHelpManager.OnActorSeen:199 hangs off TacticalLevelController.ActorSawOtherFactionActorEvent and bails on !viewer.IsFromViewerFaction — so the peer that first reveals a boss is the ONLY one whose EventTypeTriggered:53 ever runs, and the TFTV Umbra/VoidTouched/Acheron panel appeared on exactly one screen per mission (live 3-instance run 2026-08-05: an Umbra on one client, an elite on a different client, never the host). NO seq and NO ordinal: a hint is not state, it is a one-shot presentation whose only failure mode is being shown twice, and that is killed at the source by HintMirror.ShouldSend (one HashSet<string>, true exactly once per name per battle) rather than by a stream counter. WHY THE NAME AND NOT THE GUID: TFTV mints hint defs at runtime with Guid.NewGuid() (TFTVHints.cs:175/211 and CreateNewTacticalHint's callers), so a guid names a DIFFERENT def on every peer, while `name` is the string literal in TFTV's own source and is identical everywhere. The receiver resolves it through the game's OWN by-name path (the two live hint DBs, exactly as TacContextHelpManager.CmdShowHint:385-387 does) and registers it; the panel is popped by that peer's per-frame pump (law L129 arm (b)), never pushed from here, so nothing is ever put in front of a player who must dismiss it — the zero-blocking rule (L91) is preserved by construction, not by a guard. BIDIRECTIONAL, and that is the whole point: a client sends to the host, the host RELAYS to everyone (the sender cannot reach its fellow clients), and a peer that already knows the name drops it — which is also what stops the relay echoing. FAILS SOFT when the def does not exist on a peer: hints minted by host-only turn-0 code (TFTVRevenant.cs:1441/1669, TFTVAncients.cs:2478/2523, TFTVBaseDefenseTactical.cs:3503/3533, all reached only from TFTVTactical.cs:505-540 at turnNumber==0, which a client's loaded-save battle is always past) log and skip. Law L130.

        // ─── Geoscape action-relay envelope surfaces (spec 2026-07-02) — RETIRED tombstones ───
        // Were reserved for a single-surface generic intent framework (client intent → host outcome →
        // originator reject). The real framework landed 2026-07-23 as the IntentRail ENGINE instead:
        // families keep their own surface ids below (the surface byte IS the family discriminator —
        // SurfaceRouter already routes on it) and register op tables into IntentRail, which owns
        // nonce/dedup/dispatch. GeoOutcome/GeoReject never materialized on the wire: outcomes ride the
        // normal rail diff (0xAC) / the 0xAD order channel, rejects ride scoped DiffEngine
        // re-emits (IntentRail.Reject). Do not reuse these three ids.
        public const byte GeoIntent = 0xA2;   // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoOutcome = 0xA3;  // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoReject = 0xA4;   // retired: never emitted
        // ─── Old-repo per-kind geoscape mirrors — RETIRED tombstones ───
        // Ids reserved by the OLD repo's hand-sync codecs (GeoVehicleSnapshot / GeoVehicleTravelSnapshot /
        // GeoVehicleExploreSnapshot / HarvestFloatCodec / CrcProbeCodec). Never emitted by this repo — the
        // codecs were never quarried in. Their jobs ride the generic value rail 0xAC (vehicle placement /
        // travel metadata / exploration progress = covered value+order fields; harvest floats = client-side
        // presentation off its own native events). The old comments' "frozen client" premise is FALSE in
        // this repo: the client sim RUNS (clock ticks, law-4b gates skip the mutators). The CRC divergence
        // probe's job — the law-7 CRC-per-subtree backstop — landed 2026-07-29 as MsgCrcReport on the value
        // rail 0xAC itself (no side channel: DiffEngine.RootCrc / HandleCrcReport). Do not reuse these five ids.
        public const byte GeoVehiclePos = 0xA5;      // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoVehicleTravel = 0xA6;   // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoVehicleExplore = 0xA7;  // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoHarvestFloat = 0xA8;    // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoCrcProbe = 0xA9;        // retired: never emitted
        public const byte GeoResearchIntent = 0xAB;  // Research rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][factionGuid][researchId][pos:i32], op = start/cancel/front/up/down/insertAt); nonce rides the peer-aware IntentDedup; host validates + executes NATIVELY, outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail
        public const byte GeoRail = 0xAC;  // THE generic value rail (laws 5/6): host→all canonical metadata-guided diff deltas (inner = DiffEngine [MsgDelta:u8][seq:u32][kindDefs][entries], seq rides SurfaceSeq); client→host full-resend request on seq gap (inner = [MsgResyncRequest:u8], law 7 resync-on-gap); client→host per-root subtree CRC (inner = [MsgCrcReport:u8][rootKey][crc:u32][appliedSeq:u32], law 7 drift backstop — one root per second, host compares only QUIESCENT roots and answers with a scoped ForceReemit)
        public const byte GeoManufacture = 0xAD;  // Manufacturing queue rail (migration #3) host→all ORDER snapshot: [seq:u32][count:u16][(itemDefGuid, accumulatedPoints:float)×N]; the un-keyable _queue (dup defs → excluded from the generic rail) is carried by explicit order, seq rides SurfaceSeq
        public const byte GeoManufactureIntent = 0xAE;  // Manufacturing rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][itemDefGuid][index:i32], op = queue/cancel/front/up/down/scrap/scrapVehicle/quickProduce + cart ops cartAdd/cartRemove/cartScrap ([defGuid][isVehicle], cartScrap body unused); for scrap the index slot carries the item COUNT); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates (def-at-index still matches, or storage has count) + executes NATIVELY, queue outcome returns via 0xAD, scrap/buy outcome via the storage + wallet 0xAC value rail, cart outcome via mod root "M#cart" on 0xAC. (Equip-family ops moved to 0xB3 GeoEquipIntent 2026-07-26 — the surface byte IS the family.)
        public const byte GeoPersonnelIntent = 0xAF;  // Personnel progression rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][charId:i32][body], op = spendStats(str,will,speed INCREMENTS — the module's own numbers are bonus-inflated display values, only their delta is model-level) / buyAbility(trackSource,slotLevel,buttonLevel,abilityGuid) / secondSpec(specGuid) / reassign(dstRef) / skillReset(no body — free respec, host's own _hasSkillReset gates it) / hire(siteRef,vehicleRef — haven recruit, host re-derives cost/recruit) / fire(charId — KillCharacter Dismissed + vehicle scrap tail) / hireNaked(name,templateGuid,level,siteRef — content key, descriptors have no id) / tftvRedeploy(siteRef) / tftvTrainDeploy(early:bool) / tftvPromote(siteRef,specGuid — civilian→operative creation, host runs TFTV's own method) / customize(charId,[n:u8][(fieldIdx:u16, rail leaf)×n] — the WHOLE CharacterIdentity leaf set driven by its own RailType table, so colours/pattern/hair/face/sex/name ride with no field names on the wire and a cleared slot rides as a real null; captured POSTFIX on the game's own funnels GeoCharacter.RefreshTags:568 (every customization gesture on both the soldier and the vehicle screen, via UIStateUnitCustomization:60→:132) and GeoCharacter.Rename:825, host lands the leaves + runs the game's own RefreshTags)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates + re-derives the SP/mutagen cost from its OWN numbers + executes NATIVELY (ModifyBaseStat / LearnAbility / AddAbility / AddSecondaryClass / ResetCharacterProgression / the TFTV statics), outcome returns via the generic value rail 0xAC. Intent-only surface — there is no host→all personnel message.
        public const byte GeoTimeIntent = 0xB0;  // Geoscape time-control client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][val:u8], op = pause(val 0/1) / speed(val = TimeControlModule preset index — mod parity makes the index the stable id)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host applies NATIVELY (GeoscapeView.SetGamePauseState / UIModuleTimeControl.SelectTimePreset), outcome returns to all via root "T" Paused/Scale leaves + the "TA" TimeAnchor on 0xAC (same-frame: the setters raise EffectiveScaleChangedEvent → DiffEngine change-driven flush). op 3 (window hold) is DEAD and stays dead — a hold set that vetoed everyone's resume until every peer dismissed its window froze the campaign around one open popup (RailCheck L26). A blocking window is a ONE-SHOT pause issued by the GAME (GeoscapeViewSwitchQuery.ProcessQueriedStateSwitch:58-73 → RequestGamePause → SetGamePauseState), captured as an ordinary op 1; a resume from ANY peer is applied unconditionally. Intent-only surface — there is no host→all time message.
        public const byte GeoBaseIntent = 0xB1;  // Base-construction client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][siteId:i32][body], op = build(facilityDefGuid,posX:i32,posY:i32) / demolish(facilityId:u32 — the UI scrap/cancel-construction path, host refunds natively) / repair(facilityId:u32) / togglePower(facilityId:u32 — toggle only, host re-derives the target value from its OWN IsPowered + Stats.RemainingPower)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates (CanBuildFacility + CanPlaceFacility / CannotDemolish / CanRepairFacility / PowerCost + RemainingPower) + executes NATIVELY (GeoPhoenixBase.ConstructFacility/RemoveFacility(scrap:true)/RepairFacility, GeoPhoenixFacility.SetPowered), outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail (facility values keyed by FacilityId) + the structural create/destroy applier. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoEquipIntent = 0xB3;  // Soldier-equip rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][body], EquipSync owns capture+validation+native replay; op bytes kept from their 0xAE tenure — never renumber a live op: setItems=8([charId][flags][slot triples] — per-gesture loadout commit), augment=10([defGuid][charId] — augment install, bought via Wallet.Take), scrapEquipped=12([charId][list][defGuid][count][charges][amount] — per-INSTANCE scrap of an equipped item)); nonce rides IntentDedup; outcome returns via the 0xAC value rail (GeoCharacter EntityLists + storage GeoItemDict + wallet). Split off 0xAE 2026-07-26 (the surface byte IS the family). Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoEventIntent = 0xB4;  // Geoscape EVENT-WINDOW client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][eventId:string][choiceIndex:i32], op = answer(1)); choiceIndex indexes GeoscapeEventData.Choices — def order, mod parity makes the index the stable id and CompleteEvent itself works in indices (GeoscapeEvent.cs:97) — with -1 = the native "no choice" resolution (UIModuleSiteEncounters.cs:562-566); nonce rides IntentDedup; the host arbitrates FIRST-CHOICE-WINS off the REPLICATED ledger (GeoscapeEventRecord.State must still be Triggered), pays the choice cost from its own wallet and runs GeoscapeEvent.CompleteEvent NATIVELY; outcome returns via the record's own leaves + every reward-touched root on the 0xAC value rail. Intent-only surface. (The vehicle family drafted 0xB4 for itself before the event family took it; it moved to 0xB5.)
        public const byte GeoEventRaise = 0xB6;  // Geoscape EVENT-WINDOW host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][eventId:string][siteRef:string][vehicleRef:string][title:string][narrative:string], seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND window, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). Refs are ordinary rail root refs (IdentityResolver.RootRef/Resolve — "S#<siteId>" / "V#<id>@<ownerFactionGuid>"), "" = the host's own context had none. This exists because GeoscapeEventRecord carries NO site and NO context (only id/timestamps/state/_selectedChoice/_triggerCount) while every [HavenName]/[AircraftName] token dereferences GeoscapeEventContext.Site/.Vehicle UNGUARDED — so a window can only be rebuilt from what the host held AT RAISE TIME, never from the mirrored record. Texts are the host's resolved Title.Localize() / Description.Last().GetText(context); the client prefers its OWN def wherever that def resolves non-empty (player's locale, no shared-def mutation) and stamps the wire string only where the local key is empty — the TFTV runtime-narrative case (VoidOmen_{0..19}). Host→all only; the answer travels back on 0xB4, and the record's own 0xAC leaves are the dismiss signal (no dismiss message exists).
        public const byte GeoModalRaise = 0xB7;  // Geoscape QUEUED-WINDOW host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][kind:u8][modalType:i32][shape:u8][ref:string][n:u16][key:string × n][num:i32][priority:i32]. `kind` (added 2026-08-05) is the GENERIC axis: 0 = UIStateGeoModal (the only value until then, so `modalType` is read for that kind alone), 1 = UIStateAssetDeployment — the same seq stream, park queue, refusal contract, coverage gate and priority ordering now raise a NON-MODAL queued GeoscapeViewState, which was outside this surface entirely and therefore reached ONE screen. Shape 4 AssetDeploy carries GeoDeployAssetFactionCharacterBind: ref = the asset's own root ref or "" (a manufactured aircraft has no GeoCharacter — GeoscapeView.cs:1312 passes character null), keys = {aircraftDefGuid, relatedItemDefGuid} with "" for absent, num = bit1 Manufactured | bit2 NotEnoughSpace; Faction never rides (the peer stamps its own exactly as UIStateAssetDeployment.RestoreContext:35 does). Its ANSWER is host-authoritative and rides back as 0xB9 op 2 (deploy), seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND window, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). `shape` describes the host's `object modalData` by its RUNTIME type, never by a static ModalType→class table (the game's own mapping ends in a fallback chain, GetMissionBriefModal:1723): None (no data) / ResearchComplete (ref = the research's faction root, keys[0] = ResearchID, num = SwitchToResearchState) / DiplomacyReward (ref = Faction, keys = the shared ResearchIDs, num = DiplomacyShareLevel). Refs are ordinary rail root refs (IdentityResolver.RootRef/Resolve); the client re-fetches LIVE elements from its own mirrored containers, so NO text and NO def state rides the wire and each peer renders in its own locale. `priority` is the host's own queue priority, verbatim (raisers really differ: research 99, diplomacy 100, base-activated 0). Host→all only: the client's copy is built with a NULL DialogCallback and is never marked Persistent, so no button on it can run game logic and a reload cannot restore the game's own authoritative callback (UIStateGeoModal.RestoreContext:36) — hence there is no answer surface and no dismiss message, each peer closing its own copy. Which ModalTypes ride here is declared, totally, in GeoWindowCoverage.DeclaredModals (RailCheck L49).
        public const byte GeoMissionIntent = 0xB8;  // MISSION-LAUNCH client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][siteRef:string][n:u16][charRef:string × n], op = launch(1)). The wire carries IDENTITY ONLY — the mission is read off the HOST's own `site.ActiveMission` (a client's GeoMission is a structural mirror, and a wire reference could name one the host already cancelled) and the squad is rebuilt from the host's OWN GeoCharacter instances, since GeoSquad.Units holds live refs. Captured block-first at the ONE model funnel GeoMission.Launch(GeoSquad):226, which every route into a battle reaches (UIStateRosterDeployment.DeploySquad:331, GeoscapeView.LaunchMission:1043 incl. its skip-deployment arm, HavenFacilityController:149, HavenInteractionController:226, UIModuleSiteEncounters:612, StealAircraftAbility:93) — so the client never runs PrepareTacticalGame, never stamps GlobalTime and never rolls GenerateMissionThreatLevel. Host validates off REPLICATED state (MissionSync.Validate — site resolves, ActiveMission runnable, every chosen soldier resolves and is on the shared player faction, one standalone actor present, OccupingSpace sum within MissionDef.MaxPlayerUnits, at most one vehicle/mutog) + runs mission.Launch NATIVELY; the OUTCOME is not a delta at all — it is the native save transfer that pulls every peer into the battle (law 1, TacticalEntry + SaveTransferCoordinator). The CANCEL half of the same screen takes a GATE instead of an op (MissionCancelGate): backing out is navigation, and one peer must not delete a mission for everyone. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoWindowIntent = 0xB9;  // WINDOW QUEUE: ordinary client→host intents keep ops 1/2; durable mission-offer authority returns host→all as SyncKind.StateDelta with reserved bounded inner op 0x44 (offer, successor, full HostOrderKey, tombstone revision, Superseded). The delta is authenticated against HostPeerId and removes only the exact predecessor carriers. No ready/quorum state rides either direction.
        public const byte GeoCutsceneRaise = 0xBA;  // GEOSCAPE CUTSCENE host→all RAISE — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][cutsceneDefGuid:string][priority:i32], seq rides SurfaceSeq (a re-delivered raise is a SECOND cinematic, so the strictly-greater guard is what makes the surface idempotent). This is the RAISE half GeoWindowCoverage declared owed for UIStateGeoCutscene: a story cinematic fired on the host and appeared on NO other peer at all, not even in their window history, because every raiser reaches GeoscapeView directly and nothing on the wire named it. The video is REPLAYED, never shipped — each peer runs its OWN GeoscapeView.ToCutsceneState:672 off the def guid (law 2) and the host's own queue priority, so it plays from that peer's own assets and its queue orders it exactly as the host's did; no playback state and no callback crosses. ToCutsceneState is the ONE funnel every geoscape cinematic reaches (research TriggerCutscene GeoscapeView:2114, GeoFactionReward.Cinematic:264 = the GeoEventChoiceOutcome field, GeoMarketplace:165/:175, the campaign intro GeoLevelController:743, the console cheat :2186). ToGameOverState:662 is EXCLUDED BY CONSTRUCTION — it builds its own UIStateGeoCutscene with an EndFromGameOver callback that ENDS THE LEVEL (:666), which is a host outcome reaching every peer as the native teardown, so patching the funnel the two do NOT share keeps that carve-out mechanical instead of a runtime test. Host→all only: each peer closes its own copy, and there is no dismiss message (same contract as 0xB6/0xB7).
        public const byte GeoMissionOutcome = 0xBB;  // POST-MISSION REWARD host→all — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [seq:u32][resCount:u16]([resourceType:i32][value:f32])*[itemCount:u16]([ItemRec: defGuid,count,charges,malfunction])*[rowCount:u16]([kind:u8][refA:string][refB:string][value:i32])*, seq rides SurfaceSeq. WHY IT EXISTS: the eleven mission-outcome data binds are all the same six lines ending in RewardsController.SetReward(mission.Reward) (ScavengeOutcomeDataBind:42, AmbushOutcomeDataBind:42, …), and SetReward:97-117 reads reward.ApplyResult — the object GeoFactionReward.Apply:110-112 mints on the HOST. The resources and items themselves reach every peer as ordinary wallet/storage state on 0xAC; what does NOT is "what THIS battle gave you", a delta that lives only in that one object on a mission which leaves the graph moments later. Captured as a POSTFIX on GeoFaction.OnMissionRewardApplied (GeoFaction.cs:910, sole caller = the OnMissionCompleted delegate GeoSite:798-805 installs, one line after reward.Apply:801 — so the amount is GRANTED, not requested, and the seam is mission-specific rather than firing for every encounter reward). The client stashes it and MissionOutcomeMirror.StampMirroredOutcome hands it to its own GeoMission at GeoLevelController:703, five lines before :708 gives that mission to UIStateInitial — so each peer's OWN native modal draws the host's numbers and no rendered content crosses (same posture as 0xBA cutscenes). SCOPE, declared (WIDENED 2026-08-05 — no longer resources+items): the wire gained ONE GENERIC ROW ([kind][refA][refB][value:i32]) plus a single table (MissionOutcomeMirror.RowKinds) covering all SEVENTEEN kinds either native page draws at once, because every row UIModuleSiteEncounters.ShowReward:361-512 and RewardsController.SetReward:97-117 render is the same shape — up to two live references and an int. The references ride the rail's own stable addresses (law 2) and are RE-RESOLVED against the receiving peer's model, never shipped as objects; a row whose address does not resolve is dropped and counted out loud. STILL OUT, and only these: CapturedAliens (a GeoUnitDescriptor is a generated recruit with no id in any registry — there is nothing to address) and DestroySites / NewObjectives / PhoenixpediaEntries / StartMission / DestroyedVehicles, which neither page reads. NOTHING HERE IS APPLIED (law 3): every value already reached this peer as ordinary replicated state — this surface ships only the DISPLAY of what changed, and RailCheck L101 keeps the table honest by reading each Write lambda's IL against the declared GeoFactionRewardApplyResult member. Host→all only: each peer closes its own copy, no dismiss message (same contract as 0xB6/0xB7/0xBA).
        public const byte GeoReplenishIntent = 0xBC;  // POST-MISSION RESUPPLY screen client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][charId:i32][itemDefGuid:string], op = repair(1) / reload(2)). The screen's other writes already cross on existing families — GeoCharacter.SetItems (0xB3 equip), ItemManufacturing.ManufactureItem (0xAE), ItemStorage.RemoveItem (0xAC value rail) — so this surface exists ONLY for the two that did not. REPAIR is the serious one: GeoCharacter.RepairItem(GeoItem,bool):1387 is the sole funnel (the ItemDef overload :1424 delegates into it) and it does Faction.Wallet.Take:1402 + RestoreBodyPart:1404, so a client's repair click spent the SHARED wallet locally and never crossed — a law-3 violation that silently diverged every peer's money. RELOAD is captured at the SHARED choke point UIModuleReplenish.SingleItemReload:234 — the one method on that screen calling Wallet.Take:248 — so BOTH of its callers cross: SingleItemReloadAndRefresh:224 (one row's button) and ReplenishAll:288 (the OK button's batch loop). It sat on the outer wrapper until 2026-08-05 because only that one knew WHICH SOLDIER, which left a client's OK button spending the SHARED wallet locally on every un-full magazine in the returning squad; the soldier is one lookup away — the module's public Items list pairs each row's ReplenishmentElementController.Character with the very GeoItem instance the model list holds (matched by REFERENCE, since GeoItem.Equals:124 compares by def). CommonItemData.ModifyCharges is deliberately NOT the seam (a general charge write reached from everywhere). The wire carries the ADDRESS ONLY — the host re-derives cost from its OWN GetRepairCost over its OWN item health and its own wallet, so a client's screen never decides what a repair costs. Host validates off REPLICATED state (character resolves, is a Phoenix soldier, def resolves, the game's own affordability/Manufacturable/faction-knows-it tests) and runs the game's own RepairItem; outcome returns via the character + wallet on the 0xAC value rail. Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoEventReward = 0xBD;  // EVENT CHOICE-REWARD host→all — a PRESENTATION payload, not domain state: [eventId:string][resCount:u16]([resourceType:i32][value:f32])*[itemCount:u16]([ItemRec])*, encoded by MissionOutcomeMirror.Encode (0xBB's codec, reused verbatim — same shape, same declared scope). NO SurfaceSeq, deliberately: the payload is keyed by event id and an event resolves once, so a re-delivered reward is the SAME reward and applying it twice is applying it once. WHY IT EXISTS: GeoscapeEvent.ChoiceReward is written ONLY inside CompleteEvent (GeoscapeEvent.cs:101) and the very next line GRANTS it (:102), so a client must never run it (law 3) — leaving every non-answering peer with a null reward and an empty green list on the outcome page. (It never NRE-d: EventPopup.MarkResolvedInstance already stubbed ChoiceReward with an empty GeoFactionRewardApplyResult precisely because SetClosingEncounter:357 and SelectChoice:604 dereference it unguarded — the stub is now FILLED from this surface instead of left empty.) Captured as a POSTFIX on GeoscapeEvent.CompleteEvent — the reward does not exist until the native body has run, so this is a GRANTED amount and not a requested one, the same posture as 0xBB's OnMissionRewardApplied seam. The resources and items themselves already reached every peer as ordinary wallet/storage state on 0xAC; what does not is "what THIS choice gave you". SCOPE, declared (identical to 0xBB, and WIDENED with it 2026-08-05): the generic row block carries all seventeen kinds — diplomacy / new-unit / revealed-site / damaged-aircraft no longer render as nothing, because their LIVE refs (GeoFaction, GeoHavenLeader, GeoCharacter, GeoSite) now ride as the rail's own stable addresses and are re-resolved per peer. Still out: CapturedAliens + the five rows neither page reads (see 0xBB). RACE CLOSED 2026-08-05: a payload arriving AFTER this peer already built and stubbed the page is no longer lost for that render — EventPopup.RestampLiveInstance re-stamps the live dialog's ChoiceReward in place (the stub GeoFactionRewardApplyResult exists precisely so the page can be built before the reward lands), so the outcome list appears without a reopen. Host→all only; each peer closes its own copy.
        public const byte GeoVehicleIntent = 0xB5;  // AIRCRAFT gesture rail client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][vehicleRef:string][body], vehicleRef = the "V#<id>@<ownerFactionGuid>" ROOT KEY — id and owner ride the key, never the payload; op = travelTo(1, [siteRef:string] — the DESTINATION only, the host re-derives the route on its OWN navigation graph and calls GeoVehicle.StartTravel(List<GeoSite>)) / setEquipment(2, [wN:u16][defGuid × N][mN:u16][defGuid × M], "" = empty slot — the WHOLE weapon+module slot list, because GeoVehicle.ReplaceEquipments is the only native funnel and there is no per-slot mount/unmount entry point; the host assigns its OWN instances vehicle-first out of the slots + GeoFaction.AircraftItemStorage, so no HP/ammo rides the wire) / exploreSite(3, no body beyond the vehicleRef — the site is that vehicle's own CurrentSite, read off the HOST's graph, and the host runs GeoVehicle.StartExploringCurrentSite natively; the client's own call is blocked, or it would run a per-vehicle Timing.Start against its unfrozen clock)); nonce rides IntentDedup; host validates off REPLICATED state (VehicleSync.Validate — owner is the Phoenix faction, CanRedirect, destination resolves and is not the idle current site, docked at a functioning Phoenix base, slot counts match) + executes NATIVELY, outcome returns via the vehicle's own leaves (DestinationSites/SurfacePos/SurfaceRot/Weapons/Modules) + the storage on the 0xAC value rail. CREW boarding is NOT on this surface: GeoVehicle.AddCharacter/RemoveCharacter are already captured as the 0xAF personnel op=4 reassign (one gesture, one mechanism). Intent-only surface.
        public const byte GeoMarketplaceIntent = 0xBE;  // KAOS MARKETPLACE purchase client→host INTENT ([nonce:u32][op:u8][eventId:string][index:i32][offerKey:string]). ONE op (buy=1) for EVERY kind of goods — the shop sells items, vehicles and researches through one GeoEventChoice, so an op per kind would be a distinction the game itself does not make. The client names an ADDRESS and never a price: the HOST re-derives the cost from its own row, asks the GAME'S own eligibility test (GeoEventChoice.PassRequirements — the very predicate that greys the native button out, SiteBaseChoicesController:60) and runs the game's own three lines (UIModuleTheMarketplace.OnChoiceSelected:215/:219/:223) over a context it rebuilds the way GeoscapeView.ToMarketplace:736 does. BLOCK-FIRST and captured ABOVE the module, at TheMarketplaceChoicesController.OnButtonChoiceSelected:53 — the ONE funnel every row click reaches (the button's own ChoiceSelected event, plus the controller-pad SelectFirstChoiceOption:92) — because :215 takes the price out of the SHARED wallet two lines before the funnel that grants the goods, the same law-3 leak shape as GeoCharacter.RepairItem. The offerKey ("I:"/"U:" def guid, "R:" research id) is what makes the row index safe: a reroll in flight moves every row, and the host refuses a key mismatch rather than selling whatever now sits at that index. Reject reconverges the wallet ("F#") + the "MK" root and re-pushes 0xBF.
        public const byte GeoMarketplaceOffers = 0xBF;  // KAOS MARKETPLACE offer list host→all: [seq:u32][count:i32]([offerKey:string][price:f32])*, SurfaceSeq-guarded (a full snapshot, so an out-of-order older list must not revert a newer one). WHY IT EXISTS: the shop is a rail root ("MK") whose bridge covers 2 of 4 fields — docs/rail-baseline.txt:14-18 has MarketplaceOptions + UpdateOptionsNextUpdate EXCLUDED "bridge-unresolved", because GeoMarketplaceInstanceData names members the live class does not have (the offers live in MarketplaceChoices, a List<GeoEventChoice> of a different shape; the timer in the private _updateOptionsNextTime) — so the value rail carried the shop's mission flags and NOT ONE OFFER. The offers are rolled LOCALLY per peer (GeoMarketplace.UpdateOptions:218 is three Random.Range calls) and re-rolled on each peer's OWN sim clock (GeoLevelController:865) and after every mission (:756): the lists agree at join (the client's save is the host's, LevelStartLoadedGame:107) and diverge on the first reroll. The host therefore OWNS the roll — the client's UpdateOptions() is blocked (a client rolling its own shop mints shared state, law 3) — and ships the list it rolled; the client rebuilds each row through the game's OWN GenerateItemChoice/GenerateResearchChoice (:300/:292, the same two the load path calls), so prices, vehicle unwrapping and display text are the game's and the row renders in this peer's own locale off its own defs (law 2). Pushed on every reroll, after every purchase (host's own click AND an applied client intent — the consumed row rides no other channel) and from the reject reconverge. REACTIVITY is driven from the applier, not from OpenUiRepaint: the marketplace is a QUEUED window, which the universal repaint refuses to Exit+Enter (it would replay the presentation) unless the screen declares a UiNativeRepaint.Table entry — MarketplaceSync.RepaintOpenMarketplace calls the module's own UpdateVisuals:161 + SetEncounter:188 instead, never ShowEncounter (that re-posts the shop's opening narration on every push).

        // ─── Diagnostic band 0xC0-0xCF — MEASUREMENT ONLY, never domain state ───
        public const byte ClockPhaseProbe = 0xC0;  // GEOSCAPE CLOCK PHASE PROBE host→all, DIAGNOSTIC: [hostNow:f64 game seconds][hostEffectiveScale:f64][hostLatchAge:f64, -1 = never latched]. OFF unless MpDiag.On (env MULTIPLAYER_DIAG), so a normal session neither sends nor pays for it. It exists because the client-vs-host clock phase error is otherwise INVISIBLE — TimeAnchor.EnforceDrift compares the client against its OWN anchor derivation and never against the host, so both peers agree with the anchor while disagreeing with each other and all three logs of the 2026-08-04 23:22 session carry not one TimeAnchor line while the bias was present throughout. The client samples its own clock AT RECEIPT and prints one greppable [MP][clockphase] line; NOTHING is applied, no clock is written and no anchor is touched, so the surface is behaviour-neutral by construction (RailCheck L153 asserts that mechanically). It rides its OWN id rather than a field on TimeAnchor's TimingInstanceData because that DTO is the game's own and has no timestamp field (TimeAnchor.cs:97-100) — widening it would change the NORMAL session's wire, which a measurement may not do. No SurfaceSeq: the transport is per-peer ordered and a stale probe is one bad log sample, not a state write. The reading includes this packet's own one-way flight and cannot subtract it (there is no ping/pong estimator here, deliberately) — see ClockPhaseDiag's class doc for the full list of what the number cannot see.
    }
}
